WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.250
Your first day at any Allen site follows the same

00:07.250 --> 00:09.763
pattern, from the minute you arrive to the minute

00:09.763 --> 00:13.025
you leave. All of our sites run day shifts only,

00:13.025 --> 00:15.662
with no night shifts. When you arrive,

00:15.662 --> 00:18.513
check in on the tag board. Move your own tag and

00:18.513 --> 00:21.512
only your own. Visitors have a separate check-in,

00:21.512 --> 00:24.088
and at Boonesboro so do contractors.

00:24.088 --> 00:26.075
Never tag in for anyone else.

00:27.217 --> 00:30.354
Before you start any work, report to your supervisor

00:30.354 --> 00:33.092
or the designated lead. Find out where you're working

00:33.092 --> 00:35.454
today, who you're working with, and how to reach

00:35.454 --> 00:38.129
your lead by radio. Ask where your gathering point

00:38.129 --> 00:40.467
is and what your escapeways are if you're going

00:40.467 --> 00:43.342
underground. Nobody expects a new miner to know

00:43.342 --> 00:45.742
the site. They expect you to ask.

00:46.854 --> 00:49.667
Check your PPE before you leave the parking area.

00:49.667 --> 00:52.892
Hard hat, safety glasses, steel-toe boots,

00:52.892 --> 00:55.292
high-visibility clothing where required,

00:55.292 --> 00:57.767
and hearing protection, which is required around

00:57.767 --> 00:59.954
the plant while it's running and around the mine

00:59.954 --> 01:03.879
fans. If you're going underground, carry your W65

01:03.879 --> 01:06.566
self-rescuer and check it as you were shown.

01:06.566 --> 01:09.492
Wear any task-specific PPE your lead tells you

01:09.492 --> 01:12.992
to, including respiratory protection when directed.

01:12.992 --> 01:15.779
Damaged or missing PPE gets replaced before you

01:15.779 --> 01:17.479
start, not after.

01:20.800 --> 01:23.688
This is the most important rule of your first weeks.

01:23.688 --> 01:27.950
Under 30 CFR 48.7, you don't do a new task until

01:27.950 --> 01:30.650
you've been trained for it. For mobile equipment,

01:30.650 --> 01:33.438
drills, haulage and conveyor systems,

01:33.438 --> 01:35.838
ground control machines and blasting,

01:35.838 --> 01:38.350
that training covers the health and safety aspects

01:38.350 --> 01:41.650
and safe operating procedures, given on the job,

01:41.650 --> 01:44.175
plus supervised practice when production isn't

01:44.175 --> 01:47.963
the main goal, or supervised operation during production.

01:47.963 --> 01:49.875
Until you've shown the operator that you can do

01:49.875 --> 01:53.300
it safely, you run that machine only under direction

01:53.300 --> 01:56.575
and immediate supervision. For any other new task,

01:56.575 --> 01:59.312
you're instructed in its safety and health aspects

01:59.312 --> 02:01.950
and safe work procedures before you do it,

02:01.950 --> 02:04.150
including the hazards of chemicals in your work

02:04.150 --> 02:07.113
area. If someone asks you to run a machine or do

02:07.113 --> 02:10.013
a job you haven't been trained on, say so.

02:10.013 --> 02:12.975
That's not refusing work. It's following the rule.

02:14.098 --> 02:16.511
Training is given by a qualified trainer,

02:16.511 --> 02:18.898
a supervisor experienced in the task,

02:18.898 --> 02:21.036
or another person experienced in it.

02:21.036 --> 02:24.211
Watch how they do it, ask why, and don't take shortcuts

02:24.211 --> 02:26.898
you see someone else take. On a new site,

02:26.898 --> 02:29.161
the questions you ask in your first week are the

02:29.161 --> 02:31.561
ones that keep you from getting hurt in your first

02:31.561 --> 02:32.323
month.

02:33.449 --> 02:35.561
When you're trained and assigned to run mobile

02:35.561 --> 02:38.411
equipment, inspect it before you put it in operation

02:38.411 --> 02:41.711
that shift. At Allen, that's the Quarry Equipment

02:41.711 --> 02:44.461
Pre-Operational Inspection, turned in at the scale

02:44.461 --> 02:47.186
house or on the app. Check the horn,

02:47.186 --> 02:49.924
the backup alarm, the brakes, the lights and the

02:49.924 --> 02:53.236
tires. If you find a defect that affects safety,

02:53.236 --> 02:56.461
report it, and don't run equipment that isn't safe.

02:57.587 --> 03:00.312
Parts of your training can't be done on a screen.

03:00.312 --> 03:03.574
Hands-on W65 donning practice and the hands-on

03:03.574 --> 03:06.299
first-aid skills are done with the safety manager,

03:06.299 --> 03:09.424
the same day as the online course. Your hands-on

03:09.424 --> 03:13.212
W65 practice is recorded on your new miner training

03:13.212 --> 03:16.024
record. Don't go underground until it's done.

03:17.177 --> 03:19.740
Things change during a shift. Ground conditions

03:19.740 --> 03:22.902
change, equipment gets moved, a job turns out to

03:22.902 --> 03:24.765
be different from what you were told.

03:24.765 --> 03:26.790
When something changes and you're not sure it's

03:26.790 --> 03:30.302
still safe, stop and ask your lead before you continue.

03:30.302 --> 03:32.702
A good crew would rather answer a question than

03:32.702 --> 03:34.540
fill out an accident report.

03:37.863 --> 03:39.863
If you see something that could hurt someone,

03:39.863 --> 03:42.175
report it right away to your supervisor or the

03:42.175 --> 03:44.887
designated safety contact. Don't wait until the

03:44.887 --> 03:47.238
end of the shift, and don't assume someone else

03:47.238 --> 03:50.175
already reported it. If it's an immediate danger,

03:50.175 --> 03:52.475
keep people away from it while you report.

03:52.475 --> 03:54.488
You can always report a hazard by telling your

03:54.488 --> 03:57.963
supervisor. Equipment defects also go on the pre-trip

03:57.963 --> 04:01.613
inspection list or app. Supervisors record hazards

04:01.613 --> 04:04.213
on their daily inspection sheets, and anyone can

04:04.213 --> 04:07.800
log a hazard as an observation in the HCSS app.

04:07.800 --> 04:10.213
Allen's own safety rules are written in the Quarry

04:10.213 --> 04:12.825
Safety Policies, and your instructor will go over

04:12.825 --> 04:15.438
them with you. You have the right to report hazards

04:15.438 --> 04:17.775
without being punished for it. That's covered in

04:17.775 --> 04:18.938
Module 2.

04:20.060 --> 04:22.798
On your first day, find three things:

04:22.798 --> 04:25.935
the SDS binder, which is in the breakroom at every

04:25.935 --> 04:28.985
site; the nearest eyewash; and the nearest fire

04:28.985 --> 04:31.810
extinguisher, both where you work and on the equipment

04:31.810 --> 04:32.598
you run.

04:33.710 --> 04:36.810
At the end of the shift, tag out. Every time.

04:36.810 --> 04:39.210
A tag left on the board means someone might go

04:39.210 --> 04:42.223
looking for you underground. Then get home safely.

04:42.223 --> 04:46.197
At Dix River, both reportable injuries since 2018

04:46.197 --> 04:48.873
happened stepping down at the end of a shift.

04:48.873 --> 04:51.785
Face the truck, keep three points of contact,

04:51.785 --> 04:54.723
use the handrails on steps, and remember the shift

04:54.723 --> 04:56.735
isn't over until you're in your vehicle.

04:57.861 --> 05:01.173
Tag in. Report to your lead. Check your PPE.

05:01.173 --> 05:03.498
Do only the tasks you've been trained for.

05:03.498 --> 05:06.010
Report hazards right away. And tag out at the end

05:06.010 --> 05:09.286
of the day. Do those six things every shift,

05:09.286 --> 05:11.635
and your first day sets the pattern for every day

05:11.635 --> 05:12.560
after it.

05:13.682 --> 05:16.844
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

05:16.844 --> 05:18.344
override this video.
